---
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';

const addRoute = `// 1. routes/projects.js — declare the path, attach middleware
router.get('/:id', checkObjectPermission('read'), getProject);

// 2. app.js — mount it under the version prefix
app.use(\`\${API_V1_PREFIX}/projects\`, projectRoutes);

// 3. controllers/projectController.js — HTTP in, HTTP out
const getProject = asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
  const project = await projectService.byId(req.tenantId, req.params.id);
  if (!project) throw new AppError('Project not found.', 404);
  sendSuccess(res, { data: project });
});`;
---

<MainLayout
  title="API Design"
  description="The conventions behind the REST surface, and how to add an endpoint that matches them."
>
  <h1>API Design</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The API is metadata-driven: rather than generating an endpoint per object, one
    set of record endpoints is parameterised by <code>objectApiName</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Metadata-driven, not generated</h2>
  <p>
    A new custom object gets a full CRUD surface the moment it exists — no code
    generation, no deployment, no migration. The cost is that request and response
    bodies are open shapes: what keys a record has depends on metadata the server
    holds, so a static schema can only describe the envelope, not the payload.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>This is why the OpenAPI description marks records as
    <code>additionalProperties: true</code>.</strong> To discover a specific
    object's fields, call <code>GET /objects/:apiName</code>, which returns its
    <code>fields</code> array.
  </div>

  <h2>Conventions</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Convention</th><th>Rationale</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Version in the path (<code>/api/v1</code>)</td>
          <td>A future version can run beside the current one; no header negotiation.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>One envelope for every response</td>
          <td>A client checks <code>success</code> once, rather than inferring from shape.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Plural, kebab-case resources</td>
          <td><code>/list-views</code>, <code>/api-keys</code>, <code>/email-templates</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Nesting only for genuine ownership</td>
          <td>Fields nest under objects (<code>/objects/:apiName/fields</code>) because they cannot exist alone. Records don't nest under objects.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Tenancy from the credential, never the path</td>
          <td>No caller can address another tenant by constructing a URL.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>404 rather than 403 across tenants</td>
          <td>A 403 would confirm the id exists somewhere.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>PATCH partial, PUT full</td>
          <td>Both validate against the merged record, so neither can bypass a rule.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Layering</h2>
  <p>Each request passes through the same layers, in order:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Rate limiter</strong> — per IP for auth, per tenant+user otherwise.</li>
    <li><strong><code>requireAuth</code></strong> — API key, then session.</li>
    <li><strong><code>enforceTenant</code></strong> — resolves and pins the active tenant.</li>
    <li><strong>Permission check</strong> — <code>checkObjectPermission</code> or <code>checkAppPermission</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Joi validation</strong> — where a schema is declared.</li>
    <li><strong>Controller</strong> — orchestrates services and shapes the response.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="warning">
    <strong>Joi runs with <code>stripUnknown: true</code>.</strong> A key not declared
    in the schema is silently removed before the controller sees it. If a field
    you're sending vanishes, the schema is the first place to look — this has caused
    real bugs where values appeared to save and didn't. Note that
    <code>PUT /objects/:apiName</code> has no Joi schema, so it does not strip.
  </div>

  <h2>Error handling</h2>
  <p>
    Controllers throw <code>AppError(message, status)</code> and are wrapped in
    <code>asyncHandler</code>, so a rejected promise reaches the central error
    middleware rather than hanging the request. Validation collects every problem
    before responding instead of failing on the first.
  </p>

  <h2>Adding an endpoint</h2>
  <pre><code>{addRoute}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li>Return through <code>sendSuccess</code> so the envelope stays uniform.</li>
    <li>Use <code>paginate()</code> for any list.</li>
    <li>Scope every query by <code>req.tenantId</code> — it is the only thing keeping tenants apart.</li>
    <li>Put domain logic in a service, so it can be tested without an HTTP layer.</li>
    <li>Declare a Joi schema for any body you accept, remembering <code>stripUnknown</code>.</li>
    <li>Document it in <code>docs/src/data/openapi.js</code>, or it won't appear in the explorer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Known rough edges</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Most mounts have no Joi schema, so validation lives in controllers instead —
      inconsistent, and the reason a couple of fields were historically dropped
      silently.
    </li>
    <li>
      There is no cross-object query syntax. Denormalise with a rollup, or make two
      requests.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>routes/tenants.js</code> exists but is never mounted in
      <code>app.js</code> — dead code rather than a hidden endpoint.
    </li>
    <li>
      Roughly three quarters of mounted routes are undocumented. They work; their
      shapes just aren't described. See the list on
      <a href="/api/overview">API Overview</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</MainLayout>
